{"hemiparesis":{
    "Does this patient have weakness on one side of their body? For instance, the note may mention hemiparesis, unilateral paresis, unilateral weakness, or asymmetric weakness. If the text mentions weakness but does not specify that it is unilateral, respond \"not mentioned\".":"hemiparesis"
},
"nih_stroke":{
    "Is this patient showing a decreased level of consciousness? For instance, they may not be alert, may only arousable by repeated stimulation to attend, or be obtunded and require strong or painful stimulation to make movements. They may also respond only with reflex motor or autonomic effects, or be totally unresponsive, flaccid, or areflexic.":"1a level of consciousness",
    "Is the patient unable to correctly state their age and the current month if asked? For instance, they may answer one or both questions incorrectly, not respond, or be unable to respond.":"1b level of consciousness",
    "Is the patient unable to blink their eyes and squeeze their hand if asked? ":"1c level of consciousness",
    "Does this patient show abnormal eye movement? For instance, they may show partial or forced gaze palsy, a peripheral nerve paresis, or forced deviation":"2 best gaze",
    "Does this patient have hemianopia, quadrantanopia, or other vision loss due to stroke? Ignore vision loss due to age, macular degeneration, or eye damage.":"3 visual",
    "Is the patient unable to make facial movements such as showing their teeth, raising their eyebrows, or closing their eyes? For instance, the text may mention flattened nasolabial fold, asymmetry on smiling, absence of facial movement, or partial or complete facial paralysis or palsy.":"4 facial palsy",
    "Is the patient unable to keep their left arm raised for at least 10 seconds? For instance, their arm may drift down slowly or fall completely.":"5a left motor arm",
    "Is the patient unable to keep their right arm raised for at least 10 seconds? For instance, their arm may drift down slowly or fall completely.":"5a right motor arm",
    "Is the patient unable to keep their left leg raised for at least 5 seconds? For instance, their leg may drift down slowly or fall completely.":"6a left motor leg",
    "Is the patient unable to keep their right leg raised for at least 5 seconds? For instance, their leg may drift down slowly or fall completely.":"6a right motor leg",
    "Does this patient show dysmetria, oscillating movement, termor, or segmented movement of the arm or hand when performing the finger-nose maneuver on the left side? Ignore any slowness.":"7a right finger-nose",
    "Does this patient show dysmetria, oscillating movement, termor, or segmented movement of the arm or hand when performing the finger-nose maneuver on the right side? Ignore any slowness.":"7b left finger-nose",
    "Does this patient have difficulty performing the heel-shin maneuver on their left side, such as lowering their heel jerkily, or with lateral movements?":"7c left heel–shin",
    "Does this patient have difficulty performing the heel-shin maneuver on their right side, such as lowering their heel jerkily, or with lateral movements?":"7d right heel–shin",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with sensory perception? For instance, the patient may report that a pinprick is not sharp or not painful, or not be aware of being touched.":"8 sensation",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with understanding or making language or speech? For instance, they may struggle to describe a a picture, to name items, or read from a list of sentences, or they may show a loss of fluency, lack of comprehension, or limitation on ideas expressed. Ignore speech issues which are exclusively motor, such as dysarthria.":"9 language/aphasia",
    "Does this patient's speech show dysarthria, or slurring to the point that some words are not intelligible? For instance, the text may mention having to ask the patient to repeat themselves multiple times. Do not consider hypophonia.":"10 dysarthria",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with extinction and inattention? For instance, they may show visual, tactile, auditory, spatial, or personal inattention, extinction to bilateral simultaneous stimulation, hemi-inattention or extinction, or orient to only one side of space.":"11 extinction and inattention"
},
"depression":{
    "Does this patient show lack of pleasure from activities or interactions that used to be enjoyable? If the text does not mention enjoyment of activities, respond \"not mentioned\"":"anhedonia",
    "Does this patient show negative attitudes or pessimism about the future and a sense of hopelessness? If the text does not mention pessimism or hopelessness, respond \"not mentioned\"":"pessimism",
    "Is this patient uneasy or generally dissatisfied with life? If the text does not mention depressive symptoms, respond \"not mentioned\"":"dissatisfaction",
    "Does this patient seem depressed, or do they have a depression diagnosis? If the text does not mention depression or overall mood, respond \"not mentioned\"":"depressed",
    "Is this patient being treated for depression? For instance, the text may mention the patient being on antidepressant medication or receiving therapy for depression. If the text does not mention treatment, respond \"not mentioned\"":"depression treatment"
},
"memory":{
    "Does this patient show signs of acute episodic anterograde amnesia? For instance, they may start having difficulty remembering events that happened after a stroke, or be unable to form new memories after the stroke. If the text mentions short-term memory problems only, respond \"not mentioned\".":"anterograde_amnesia",
    "Does this patient show signs of acute episodic retrograde amnesia? For instance, they may have difficulty remembering events that occured before a stroke. If the text mentions short-term memory problems only, or memory problems not associated with a stroke, respond \"not mentioned\".":"retrograde_amnesia"
},
"bars":{
    "Does this patient have difficulty walking or disturbed gait? For instance, they may mention the patient staggering, difficulties in half turn, requiring support from a wall or stick, or being entirely unable to walk on their own. If the text does not mention walking or gait, or only mentions gait changes caused by pain or injury, respond \"not mentioned\"":"gait",
    "Does this patient have difficulty performing the heel-shin maneuver such as lowering their heel jerkily, or with lateral movements?":"heel–shin",
    "Does this patient's speech show dysarthria, or slurring to the point that some words are not intelligible? For instance, the text may mention having to ask the patient to repeat themselves multiple times. Do not consider hypophonia.":"speech",
    "Does this patient have oculomotor abnormalities? For example, the text may mention slowed pursuit, saccadic intrusions, hypo/hypermetric saccade, or nystagmus.":"oculomotor",
    "Does this patient show dysmetria, oscillating movement, termor, or segmented movement of the arm or hand when performing the finger-nose maneuver? Ignore any slowness.":"finger-nose"
    },
"bars_severity":{
    "Which of these most closely describes the patient's gait? If the text does not mention walking or gait, or only mentions gait changes caused by pain or injury, respond \"-1\" -1: No information about the patient's gait provided. 0: Normal. 1: Almost normal naturally, but unable to walk with feet in tandem position. 2: Walking without support, but clearly abnormal and irregular. 3: Walking without support but with considerable staggering; difficulties in half turn. 4: Walking without support not possible; uses support of the wall for 10-meter test. 5: Walking possible only with one cane. 6: Walking possible only with two canes or with a stroller. 7: Walking possible only with one accompanying person. 8: Walking impossible with one accompanying person (2-person assist; wheelchair)": "gait",
    "Which of these most closely describes how the patient performs the heel-shin/knee-tibia test?. -1: No information about the heel-shin/knee-tibia test provided. 0: Normal. 1: Lowering of heel in continuous axis, but movement is decomposed in several phases, without real jerks, or abnormally slow. 2: Lowering jerkily in the axis. 3: Lowering jerkily with lateral movements. 4: Lowering jerkily with extremely long lateral movements, or test impossible": "heel-shin",
    "Which of these most closely describes the patient's speech? Do not consider hypophonia. -1: No information about speech ability or dysarthria provided. 0: Normal. 1: Mild impairment of rate/rhythm/clarity. 2: Moderate impairment of rate/rhythm/clarity. 3: Severely slow and dysarthric speech. 4: Speech absent or unintelligible": "speech",
    "Which of these most closely describes the patient's oculomotor ability?. Ignore any slowness. -1: No information about oculomotor ability provided. 0: Normal. 1: Slightly slowed pursuit, saccadic intrusions, hypo/hypermetric saccade, nystagmus. 2: Prominently slowed pursuit, saccadic intrusions, hypo/hypermetric saccade, nystagmus": "oculomotor",
    "Which of these most closely describes how the patient performs the finger-to-nose test?. -1: No information about the finger-to-nose test provided. 0: Normal. 1: Oscillating movement of arm and/or hand without decomposition of the movement. 2: Segmented movement in 2 phases and / or moderate dysmetria in reaching nose. 3: Segmented movement in more than 2 phases and / or considerable dysmetria in reaching nose. 4: Dysmetria preventing the patient from reaching nose": "finger-nose"
    },
"bars_pull_scores":{
    "What score did the clinician give this patient on the BARS questionnaire gait question? If no score is given in the text, respond \"-1\"": "gait_clinician",
    "What score did the clinician give this patient on the BARS questionnaire heel-shin test? If separate right and left values are given in the text, report both separated by a slash. If no score is given in the text, respond \"-1\"": "heel-shin_clinician",
    "What score did the clinician give this patient on the BARS questionnaire speech/dysarthria question? If no score is given in the text, respond \"-1\"": "speech_clinician",
    "What score did the clinician give this patient on the BARS questionnaire oculomotor test? If separate right and left values are given in the text, report both separated by a slash. If no score is given in the text, respond \"-1\"": "oculomotor_clinician",
    "What score did the clinician give this patient on the BARS questionnaire finger-to-nose test? If separate right and left values are given in the text, report both separated by a slash. If no score is given in the text, respond \"-1\"": "finger-nose_clinician",
    "What overall score did the clinician give this patient on the BARS questionnaire? If no score is given in the text, respond \"-1\"": "total_clinician"
    },
"moca":{
    "Does this patient have difficulty performing visuospatial or executive tasks such as a path task, copying a line drawing of a bed, or drawing a clock?":"visuospatial",
    "Does this patient struggle with naming tasks such as naming animals based on pictures?":"naming",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with memory, such as being unable to remember a list of words read to them?":"memory",
    "Does this patient have difficulty paying attention? For instance, they may be unable to repeat a list of numbers or letters read to them, either forwards or backwards, or may struggle with a serial subtraction task.":"attention",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with language skills? For instance, they may have difficulty repeating a sentence told to them. Only consider cognitive problems, and ignore dysarthria, slurring, and any other motor issues":"language",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with abstraction? For instance, they may struggle to identify similarities between a hammer and screwdriver, or matches and lamps.":"abstraction_similarities",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with delayed recall? For instance, if given a list of words, they may have difficulty repeating them back five minutes later.":"delayed_recall",
    "Does this patient have difficulty orienting themselves? For instance, they may not know the date, month, year, day, city, or place they are in.":"orientation"
    },
"ccas":{
    "Does this patient have difficulty with semantic fluency? For instance, they may not be able to name more than 15 animals in one minute.":"semantic fluency",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with phonemic fluency? For instance, they may not be able to name more than 9 words starting with a given letter out of 19.":"phonemic fluency",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with category switching? For instance, they may not be able to alternate between naming vegetables and types of jobs for more than 9 words in one minute.":"category switching",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with verbal registration? For instance, they may not be able to repeat a list of 5 words given to them within 4 attempts.":"verbal registration",
    "Does this patient have difficulty repeating strings of digits, forwards or backwards?":"digit span",
    "Is this patient unable to correctly draw a cube, with the correct number of lines?":"cube draw",
    "Is this patient unable to correctly copy a drawing of a cube?":"cube copy",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with verbal recall? For instance, if given a list of words, they may may not be able to repeat them all 5 minutes later without cues or hints.":"verbal recall",
    "Is this patient unable to identify similarities between objects, such as nose/ear, sheep/elephant, lake/river, or airplane/motorcycle?":"similarities",
    "Does this patient have difficulty completing a go-no go task, where they raise their finger if the doctor taps the table once, but not if they tap twice?":"go-no go",
    "Does this patient have unusual affect? For instance, they may have difficulty with mental flexibility; be emotionally labile or show incongruous emotions; show easy sensory overload or avoidant behaviors; express illogical thoughts; lack empathy, be apathetic, or have blunted affect; or be angry or aggressive, irritable, oppositional, with difficulty with social cues and social boundaries. Ignore depression or anxiety symptoms.":"affect"
    },
"ccas_severity":{
    "How many animals or living creatures is this patient able to name in one minute? If an animal naming task is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"semantic fluency",
    "How many words beginning with the letter F is this patient able to name in one minute? If a phonemic naming task is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"phonemic fluency",
    "How many words is this patient able to name in one minute in a category switching task, where they need to switch between naming a vegetable and a profession or job?  If a category switching task is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"category switching",
    "How many digits is this subject able to repeat forwards? If a forwards digit repetition task is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"digit span forwards",
    "How many digits is this subject able to repeat backwards? If a backwards digit repetition task is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"digit span backwards",
    "Is this patient unable to correctly draw a cube, with the correct number of lines?  Respond \"1\" for yes and \"0\" for no. If a cube draw task is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"cube draw",
    "What is this patient's score on a cube draw/cube copy task? If a cube draw or copy task is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"cube copy",
    "How many words is this patient able to remember in a verbal recall task?. If a verbal recall test is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"verbal recall",
    "How many pairs of words is the patient able to identify similarities between words on an abstraction/similarities task? If a similarities task is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"similarities",
    "Does this patient have difficulty completing a go-no go task, where they raise their finger if the doctor taps the table once, but not if they tap twice?  Respond \"1\" for yes and \"0\" for no. If a go-no go task is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"go-no go"
    },
"ccas_fail":{
    "Did this patient fail the CCAS semantic fluency task, where they are asked to name as many animals as they can in one minute? A score of 15 or less is considered a failure. Respond 1 for yes and 0 for no, and if the CCAS questionnaire is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"semantic fluency fail",
    "Did this patient fail the CCAS phonemic fluency task, where they are asked to name as many words starting with F as they can in one minute? A score of 9 or less is considered a failure. Respond 1 for yes and 0 for no, and if the CCAS questionnaire is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"phonemic fluency fail",
    "Did this patient fail the CCAS category switching task, where they need to switch between naming a vegetable and a profession or job? A score of 9 or less is considered a failure. Respond 1 for yes and 0 for no, and if the CCAS questionnaire is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"category switching fail",
    "Did this patient fail the CCAS digit span forwards task, where they are asked to repeat strings of digits read to them? A score of 5 or less is considered a failure. Respond 1 for yes and 0 for no, and if the CCAS questionnaire is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"digit span forwards fail",
    "Did this patient fail the CCAS digit span backwards task, where they are asked to repeat strings of digits read to them in reverse? A score of 3 or less is considered a failure. Respond 1 for yes and 0 for no, and if the CCAS questionnaire is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"digit span backwards fail",
    "Did this patient fail the CCAS cube draw/cube copy task, where they are asked to draw a 3D cube? A score of 12 or less is considered a failure.":"cube draw/copy fail",
    "Did this patient fail the CCAS verbal recall task? A score of 10 or less is considered a failure. Respond 1 for yes and 0 for no, and if the CCAS questionnaire is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"verbal recall fail",
    "Did this patient fail the CCAS similarities task, where they are asked to explain similarities between words? A score of 6 or less is considered a failure. Respond 1 for yes and 0 for no, and if the CCAS questionnaire is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"similarities fail",
    "Did this patient fail the CCAS go-no go task, where they raise their finger if the doctor taps the table once, but not if they tap twice? A score of 0 is considered a failure. Respond 1 for yes and 0 for no, and if the CCAS questionnaire is not mentioned, respond \"-1\"":"go-no go fail"
    },
"ccas_pull_scores":{
    "What score did the clinician give this patient on the CCAS questionnaire semantic fluency question? If no score is given in the text, respond \"-1\"":"semantic fluency clinician",
    "What score did the clinician give this patient on the CCAS questionnaire phonemic fluency question? If no score is given in the text, respond \"-1\"":"phonemic fluency clinician",
    "What score did the clinician give this patient on the CCAS questionnaire category switching question? If no score is given in the text, respond \"-1\"":"category switching clinician",
